AIR RAID — CANONICAL GAME RULES (v0.3.0)

GAME
Two local players each control an automatically moving plane. Players cannot steer.
Blue fires using Enter. Orange fires using Space.
Press/release once per shot: holding the key does not automatically fire again.
A player can have at most one active bomb. Another shot is available only after
the current bomb has hit an intact target or left the field without scoring.

TARGETS AND POINTS
There are 8 rows and 38 columns of targets. Each pair of rows is worth 1, 2,
3 or 4 points (top to bottom), for a total of four point tiers.
Each target may be destroyed once. Its points go only to the first bomb
to reach it; no target may be scored twice.
When both bombs reach the same target at exactly the same time, the priority
alternates between Blue and Orange, beginning with Blue each new match.
The player who loses the exact tie keeps the bomb in flight.

CANYON STRIKES (the original 'four canyon hits' rule)
A launched bomb that leaves the playfield by the left edge, right edge or
bottom without destroying a previously intact target causes ONE canyon strike
for the player who fired it. Successfully hitting a target causes no strike.
On the fourth strike, that player immediately loses, regardless of score.
If both players take their fourth strike during the same simulation step,
the result is a draw.

TIME LIMIT AND WINNER
Each match lasts up to 60 seconds of simulated game time. At 0 seconds,
the highest score wins; equal scores produce a tie.
A fourth canyon strike takes priority over the clock even if both occur in
the same simulation step. Events for both players are resolved before the
match outcome is decided. After the result, no further shots or score updates
are allowed.

CONTROLS AND FLOW
Enter: Blue fire.
Space: Orange fire.
P: Pause/resume the match.
R: Restart a fresh match.
Buttons: Start, Pause/Resume, Restart, Menu.
Pause freezes the match timer and plane/bomb movement.
Restart clears scores, strikes, targets, bombs, winner, and held-key state.

IMPLEMENTATION NOTE
A single Swing EDT timer drives a fixed-step model; no background worker
threads are used. Collision detection is continuous within each simulation
step to avoid passing through thin targets.

This rulebook supersedes the original ambiguous "hit the canyon 4 times"
instruction. The original project did not define whether a bomb missing
a target counted as a canyon hit, so v0.3.0 formalizes that behavior.
